      "name": null,
      "problem": "Written in the form $y=mx+b,$ what is the equation of the line that passes through the points $(6,-2)$ and $(0,2)?$\n\nThe equation of the line is $y=$\n{{field-1}}.",
      "worked_solution": "We will write the equation of the line in the slope-intercept form $y=mx+b.$ So, we need to work out the slope $m$ and the $y$-intercept $b.$\n\nFirst, we calculate the slope $m$ using the given points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2-(-2)}{0-6} \\\\  & =\\frac{2+2}{0-6} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{-6} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe coordinates of the $y$-intercept are always $(0,b).$ We know that the line passes through the point $(0,2),$ so the $y$-intercept is $b=2.$  \n\nSubstituting the slope $m=-\\frac{2}{3}$ and the $y$-intercept $b=2$ into the slope-intercept formula $y=mx+b,$ we reach\n\n$$\ny=-\\frac{2}{3}x+2.\n$$",
